langgraph-blog-generator

A minimal LangGraph example that generates a blog post in two steps: it first writes an outline for a topic, then expands that outline into a full blog post, using OpenAI's gpt-4o-mini.

How it works

The workflow is a simple sequential graph with a shared state:

START → write_outline → generate_blog → END

State (OutlineState)

Field	Type	Description
topic	str	The subject of the blog post (input)
outline	str	Outline produced by the first node
blog	str	Final blog post produced by node two

Nodes

write_outline – prompts the model to write an outline for the given topic.
generate_blog – prompts the model to write a full blog post from that outline.
Getting started
Prerequisites
Python 3.9+
An OpenAI API key
Installation
bash
git clone https://github.com/<your-username>/langgraph-blog-generator.git
cd langgraph-blog-generator

python -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate        # Windows: venv\Scripts\activate

pip install langgraph langchain-openai langchain-core python-dotenv ipython jupyter
Configuration

Create a .env file in the project root:

OPENAI_API_KEY=your_api_key_here

Never commit your .env file. Add it to .gitignore.

Run

Open the notebook and run all cells:

bash
jupyter notebook main02.ipynb

The final cells invoke the workflow and print the results:

python
ans = workflow.invoke({"topic": "Python Programming"})

print(ans["outline"])
print(ans["blog"])

Change the topic value to generate a post about anything you like.

Project structure
langgraph-blog-generator/
├── main02.ipynb    # Graph definition and example run
├── .env            # API key (not committed)
└── README.md
Tech stack
LangGraph – graph-based workflow orchestration
LangChain OpenAI – ChatOpenAI wrapper
python-dotenv – environment variable loading
Ideas for extension
Add a review/edit node that critiques and improves the draft
Add conditional edges to loop until quality criteria are met
Accept tone, audience, or word-count parameters in the state
Swap in a different model or provider
